infinite information and conflicting advice.&nbsp;
Let's begin with definitions. Could you explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:54.900
your position on — well, first defining what&nbsp;
the DSM-5 diagnostic categories are, what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.900 --> 00:13:04.740
is as a tool, and then your current evaluation&nbsp;
or perspective on DSM-5 diagnostic categories.&nbsp;

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:10.800
Dr. Christopher Palmer: DSM-5 is kind of the bible&nbsp;
of psychiatry, and it's what all mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:18.180
professionals have to use to make a diagnosis. If&nbsp;
you want to treat patients and get reimbursed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:23.880
insurance, you have to assign a diagnostic label.&nbsp;
If no label is assigned, you don't get paid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:31.260
If pharmaceutical companies want to develop pills&nbsp;
for psychiatric disorders, they have to abide by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:39.720
these labels and study pills for specific labels.
And the assumption is that these labels are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:47.820
discrete, separate illnesses, and they define&nbsp;
a set of symptoms, but more importantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:56.460
they define a prognosis and a life trajectory&nbsp;
sometimes. They also define treatments that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.460 --> 00:14:03.540
work and treatment approaches and how long people&nbsp;
might need treatment. And in many of those ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:08.640
the diagnostic labels are actually useful.&nbsp;
And I'm not here to challenge any of that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:13.800
I think understanding what symptoms people&nbsp;
are having and understanding what treatments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:18.420
might be helpful to reduce those symptoms is&nbsp;
actually really invaluable. And that's great.&nbsp;

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:27.000
But the reality is that every diagnostic&nbsp;
label in DSM is a syndrome. And what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:31.620
means is that it's just a constellation of&nbsp;
signs and symptoms that we put together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:43.740
and no one knows what causes any of them. And the&nbsp;
real answer is that when researchers have looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:50.820
at all of the diagnostic categories, it turns&nbsp;
out that there are two major problems with DSM.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:57.960
And the two major problems with DSM are&nbsp;
heterogeneity and comorbidity. And what that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:05.040
means is — heterogeneity means that if you see two&nbsp;
people with OCD or with autism spectrum disorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:10.200
they can have extraordinarily different symptoms&nbsp;
and look and function very differently from each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.200 --> 00:15:15.780
other. And it can seem like there's no way they&nbsp;
have the same brain disorder or brain illness or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:22.440
whatever we want to call it. That's heterogeneity.
The comorbidity part is even more confusing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.440 --> 00:15:26.700
Because it turns out, if you have one&nbsp;
psychiatric disorder and you're getting mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:31.740
health treatment, there is an overwhelming&nbsp;
probability that you have more than one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.340 --> 00:15:42.540
And you can mix and match them however you'd like.
And this is really important because it implies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:49.200
that maybe our diagnostic labels, number one,&nbsp;
are not valid constructs. And number two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:56.460
maybe our diagnostic labels are not as distinct&nbsp;
as we think they are. Because if I look at people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.460 --> 00:16:03.480
with eating disorders, and they're all more likely&nbsp;
to have depression and OCD, but I look at people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:09.180
with schizophrenia, and they're all more likely to&nbsp;
have depression and OCD, and I look at people with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:16.080
personality disorders, and they're more likely to&nbsp;
have depression and OCD, the boundaries between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:21.960
these diagnostic labels really start to fall&nbsp;
apart. And you start to wonder what's going on.&nbsp;

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:27.060
Much more importantly, when we look at the&nbsp;
root causes, the root causes that we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:33.540
know about for mental disorders, it turns&nbsp;
out there is no single root cause for any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:39.960
singular psychiatric diagnosis. And this goes&nbsp;
all the way down to the level of genetics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:47.880
Specific genes even do not confer risk. There&nbsp;
is no one gene that confers risk for only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:55.260
disorder like schizophrenia or bipolar or autism.
One gene confers risk for numerous different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.260 --> 00:17:00.540
disorders. So there might be one gene that&nbsp;
confers risk for schizophrenia and bipolar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:07.380
and autism and epilepsy and other diagnoses.&nbsp;
And so, even if you look at a single root cause,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:16.980
like something so concrete as a gene, they're&nbsp;
not distinct entities. And the reality is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:24.720
the National Institute of Health abandoned the&nbsp;
DSM-5 diagnoses about a decade ago, recognizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:30.480
these aren't valid constructs, and we're not&nbsp;
really getting anywhere, assuming they are.&nbsp;

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:37.800
Tim Ferriss: Man, but it's a bitch to get&nbsp;
