with mental illness. And after I went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.320 --> 00:19:34.980
that hell with her and homelessness and&nbsp;
everything else, it only spiraled down.&nbsp;

00:19:34.980 --> 00:19:43.740
And by the time that I was in my twenties, I&nbsp;
actually had gotten to a good place. I was not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.740 --> 00:19:50.340
clinically depressed anymore. I wasn't impaired.&nbsp;
I was actually getting through medical school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:55.140
I had finished medical school. Actually won&nbsp;
an award for being one of the top students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:04.260
And I was doing my psychiatry residency at&nbsp;
Harvard. And so, anybody looking from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:11.040
outside would say, "This guy's doing well enough.&nbsp;
He clearly is not impaired by a mental disorder."&nbsp;

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:16.200
But I had metabolic syndrome,&nbsp;
so blood pressure was up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:23.100
cholesterol and lipid levels were awful.&nbsp;
I had prediabetes. And year after year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:28.680
my doctor kept telling me, "Diet and exercise." I&nbsp;
was doing a super low-fat diet. I was exercising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:33.840
regularly. It was not doing anything.&nbsp;
All the numbers were just getting worse.&nbsp;

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:38.580
And he finally kept pushing meds and was like,&nbsp;
"You're going to have to go on meds." And I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:45.240
like, "Damn, dude, I'm in my twenties.&nbsp;
You're putting me on meds already? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.240 --> 00:20:51.720
that means I'm going to have a heart attack by&nbsp;
the time I turn 45. That is not okay. And I am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:58.320
not going to accept this as my final answer."&nbsp;
And so I had heard through the grapevine about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:03.420
low-carb diets. At that point, it was the Atkins&nbsp;
diet. And I had heard that it was helping some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:07.800
people reverse their diabetes or improve&nbsp;
their cholesterol levels and other things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:13.440
It went against everything I'd been&nbsp;
taught. But I decided to give it a shot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.980 --> 00:21:21.780
really just as my hail Mary pass. And within three&nbsp;
months, my metabolic syndrome was completely gone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:25.860
Everything was better. But&nbsp;
the thing that I noticed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:33.720
was I had dramatic improvement in&nbsp;
mood, energy, sleep, confidence.&nbsp;

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:42.960
I became a different person. A person I had never&nbsp;
been in my entire life, even during good times. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.960 --> 00:21:51.720
had never felt that good. And so I, of course, am&nbsp;
recommending this to friends and family who want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.720 --> 00:21:58.140
to lose weight or shed a few pounds. And some&nbsp;
of them are reporting equal results. So within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:03.360
a couple years, I start using it in patients with&nbsp;
treatment-resistant depression. And lo and behold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:07.560
it worked for at least some of them. It didn't&nbsp;
work for everyone, but it worked for some of them.&nbsp;

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:17.520
I think that pivotal moment that really changed&nbsp;
everything for me was actually in 2016 when I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:23.040
a longstanding patient, schizoaffective disorder,&nbsp;
which is cross between schizophrenia and bipolar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:29.760
And he asked for my help to lose weight.&nbsp;
He had tried 17 different medications.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:35.460
None of them worked. He was tormented&nbsp;
by his illness in similar ways to Doris.&nbsp;

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:41.700
I start this diet. Within two weeks, he's&nbsp;
losing weight, and I start to notice this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:48.900
antidepressant effect in him. I'm like, "Well,&nbsp;
that's interesting." Within six to eight weeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:53.400
he too spontaneously — it didn't&nbsp;
happen right away for him. Took six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:57.600
to eight weeks. But he spontaneously reports&nbsp;
hallucinations, delusions are going away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:03.180
That man went on to lose 160&nbsp;
pounds, has kept it off to this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:09.600
and has had a new life. Has had the ability&nbsp;
to do things he had not been able to do.&nbsp;

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:16.500
So, to get back to your question,&nbsp;
those were the serendipitous things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.500 --> 00:23:22.740
that just happened in my life. I didn't&nbsp;
set out on a path to prove the ketogenic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.740 --> 00:23:26.804
diet is a thoughtful intervention. It was —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it snuck in the side door.&nbsp;

00:23:26.804 --> 00:23:32.400
Dr. Christopher Palmer: It actually snuck in the&nbsp;
side door and smacked me in the face. I was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:38.400
"What the hell is going on here? This&nbsp;
is impossible." And for people who know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:45.000
the keto diet as a fad, weight-loss diet or&nbsp;
whatever, it is a 100-year-old evidence-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:50.040
treatment for epilepsy. And that was&nbsp;
a really important clue to me because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:50.040 --> 00:23:57.300
we use epilepsy treatments in psychiatry all&nbsp;
the time. Medications like Depakote, Tegratal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:57.300 --> 00:24:03.120
Lamictal, Topamax, Valium, Klonopin, Xanax;&nbsp;
all of those are anti-seizure treatments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:09.180
And we use them in tens if not hundreds of&nbsp;
millions of people worldwide all the time.&nbsp;

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:15.000
And so I quickly recognize, well wait,&nbsp;
if this diet actually stops seizures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:21.300
and we use anti-seizure treatments all the time,&nbsp;
maybe that's the reason it's working. And wow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:27.480
this is shocking because it's really working.
And then I learned about the decades of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.480 --> 00:24:32.640
neuroscience research that we have documenting&nbsp;
what exactly the ketogenic diet is doing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:39.960
brain. Changes neurotransmitters, changes calcium&nbsp;
channel regulation. Changes gene expression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:46.140
actually. The gut microbiome is involved,&nbsp;
inflammation. It's doing all sorts of things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:54.120
And armed with all of that information,&nbsp;
my career took off. I started doing more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:56.940
research, collaborating with researchers&nbsp;
around the world, and everything else.&nbsp;

00:24:56.940 --> 00:25:01.620
Tim Ferriss: Is it fair — this term came up&nbsp;
in the video that I watched, and in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:07.560
the conference name, I think, had at least some&nbsp;
descriptor of this: metabolic psychiatry. Is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:07.560 --> 00:25:12.756
a fair description of at least a component of&nbsp;
what you do? Or would you use a different label?&nbsp;

00:25:12.756 --> 00:25:14.640
Dr. Christopher Palmer: I&nbsp;
think metabolic psychiatry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:24.480
is definitely an emerging field. There&nbsp;
are lots of researchers, neuroscientists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:31.620
clinicians, philanthropists backing this&nbsp;
right now. The Baszucki Brain Research Fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:40.500
funded by David and Jan Baszucki, founder Roblox.&nbsp;
They are really backing this with passion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:41.400 --> 00:25:49.440
I think at the end of the day, the brain energy&nbsp;
theory is bigger than just psychiatry. The brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:57.180
energy theory is about metabolism, ultimately&nbsp;
mitochondria, and how it affects human health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:05.340
and how that can have an impact on the brain.&nbsp;
But it's more than the brain. The brain is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:11.640
part of the human body, and the human body&nbsp;
impacts the brain. And so, I'm actually more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:17.400
interested in the big picture. If you climb the&nbsp;
mountain and look down, or if you see the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.400 --> 00:26:23.460
forest from the trees, you see the big picture.
We're really talking about a new way to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:34.020
human health. And that includes mental health, but&nbsp;
it also easily segues into physical health. So my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:39.660
only qualm with metabolic psychiatry is that it's&nbsp;
focused on psychiatry. And I am a psychiatrist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:44.700
I'm passionate about helping people with mental&nbsp;
disorders, make no mistake. I'm not looking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.700 --> 00:26:49.320
start a weight-loss clinic for just any Joe or&nbsp;
Jane who want to lose weight. That's not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.320 --> 00:26:53.880
my passion. But yeah, I am going to be helping&nbsp;
people lose weight and improve their physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.880 --> 00:27:01.680
abilities and prevent cancer and prevent diabetes&nbsp;
or reverse diabetes. So it's more than psychiatry.&nbsp;

00:27:01.680 --> 00:27:08.040
Tim Ferriss: The Cartesian sort of&nbsp;
mind/body duality doesn't actually pan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:14.520
out very well when everything is connected&nbsp;
with blood vessels and so on and so forth.&nbsp;

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:20.280
So let's take a closer look at some of&nbsp;
the outcomes. And I'll share some of my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:31.560
personal history as a way of making that segue.
I have experimented with the ketogenic diet since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:40.140
'96 or '97, I would say. And initially&nbsp;
experimented with the ketogenic diet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:44.760
a cyclical ketogenic diet, with one&nbsp;
day of carbohydrate loading following a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.760 --> 00:27:49.560
glycogen-depletion workout, it's a bit of a long&nbsp;
story, for athletic purposes. I was using it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.940 --> 00:27:58.440
lose body fat and improve my body composition&nbsp;
while maintaining some degree of muscular growth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:04.380
and anabolism by spiking insulin once&nbsp;
a week with this carbohydrate loading.&nbsp;

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:11.220
And it worked spectacularly well. I was using&nbsp;
it for athletic performance. But much later now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:11.220 --> 00:28:19.800
I've done a fair amount of fasting, quite a bit of&nbsp;
ketogenic dieting. And when people ask me for fat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.800 --> 00:28:26.760
loss, there are many options. There are actually&nbsp;
quite a few really good options. But the point I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:26.760 --> 00:28:32.100
make is: if you suffer from depression or&nbsp;
anxiety, personally I have found nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.180 --> 00:28:42.060
close to as dramatic as the ketogenic diet for&nbsp;
eliciting a significant change. And it's so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:49.140
noticeable for me that if I'm using, say, an&nbsp;
Abbott Labs Precision Xtra device to measure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:57.000
ketone concentrations and millimolars,&nbsp;
predictably, based on how I feel in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.000 --> 00:29:01.980
not just mood, but mental acuity. So speed of&nbsp;
cognitive turnover. I know it's not technical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:08.520
term, but just speed of computation, short term&nbsp;
memory. I can tell when my body clicks over past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:08.520 --> 00:29:17.820
say 0.7 millimolars. Predictably, I can guess&nbsp;
where I am between 0.7 and say 1.0 millimolars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:26.460
based on mood and cognition and furthermore,&nbsp;
over the span of several days, and typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:26.460 --> 00:29:32.220
I kickstart ketosis with a period of fasting, I&nbsp;
just find that the easiest for me personally, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.220 --> 00:29:40.920
I recognize the sort of adherence challenges that&nbsp;
can pose for a lot of folks. I also seem to — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:46.080
this is leading to a question, don't worry.
I also notice that when I am in reasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.040 --> 00:29:53.220
established ketosis, because I think a lot of&nbsp;
people think they're in ketosis when they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.240 --> 00:30:02.220
eating a ketogenic meal once a day and that we&nbsp;
can get to, but when I am measurably in ketosis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:10.020
my need for sleep also goes down dramatically. And&nbsp;
I would say I wake up whether I want to or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.020 --> 00:30:14.820
after six hours of sleep and I feel fully&nbsp;
refreshed. I don't experience the morning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:23.400
grogginess that I typically do. The question&nbsp;
is what the hell is going on? So biochemically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:33.060
from a genetic on/off switch perspective, what&nbsp;
is happening in the body that contributes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:37.500
some of these experiences and effects?
Dr. Christopher Palmer: It's a brilliant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:42.720
question and I don't think we have all the&nbsp;
answers because this is a relatively new field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:42.720 --> 00:30:47.460
and in terms of looking at the effects&nbsp;
of the ketogenic diet on mental states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.020 --> 00:30:54.960
and certainly in terms of looking at&nbsp;
it in terms of people with exclusive or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.960 --> 00:30:59.640
primary mental disorders, researchers&nbsp;
have looked at it for other things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:01.140 --> 00:31:07.140
So the first thing I'll say is you're actually,&nbsp;
everything you described is spot on with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.140 --> 00:31:11.880
I see clinically in dozens of patients and&nbsp;
what I hear from patients around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:19.080
and it's one of the challenges, I want to point&nbsp;
this out, you are highlighting the decreased need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.080 --> 00:31:25.860
for sleep is one of the biggest risks of using&nbsp;
this intervention in people who have had a history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:34.620
of psychosis. And the reason is because for you,&nbsp;
it sounds like it has not been a major problem.&nbsp;

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:41.280
I've definitely seen people who have gotten&nbsp;
hypomanic while, especially while they're keto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:47.160
adapting to the point that they're actually only&nbsp;
getting two hours of sleep a night and they can't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:53.700
sleep more than that. And sometimes they recognize&nbsp;
"This may not be optimal for me." And other times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:53.700 --> 00:31:58.980
they just think, "Well, I'm got a lot of time&nbsp;
now to be productive and get all sorts of shit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:58.980 --> 00:32:05.820
done." So I mean, one guy actually was going for&nbsp;
20-mile runs starting at 3:00 a.m. in the morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:13.860
and he complained that he couldn't maintain a&nbsp;
normal weight and he wondered why. And I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:18.900
"Dude, you're like sleeping two hours a&nbsp;
night and you're running 20 miles every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:18.900 --> 00:32:22.980
what did you expect?" I don't think you —
Tim Ferriss: Some basic math&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:26.700
can explain this. Yeah, yeah.
Dr. Christopher Palmer: Yes, absolutely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:33.660
The effects on sleep are complicated because&nbsp;
some studies like in epilepsy patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.660 --> 00:32:39.300
for instance, have found that the ketogenic diet&nbsp;
actually dramatically improves sleep overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.900 --> 00:32:44.520
but a lot of those patients are sleeping too&nbsp;
much and often because they're on medications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:44.520 --> 00:32:50.340
that are extraordinarily sedating. And so those&nbsp;
studies come to the conclusion, ketogenic diet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:50.340 --> 00:32:56.340
improves sleep, it normalizes it, people wake&nbsp;
up feeling more refreshed, it's a great thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:57.300 --> 00:33:02.220
This issue of hypomania and mania is very&nbsp;
poorly understood. I'm one of the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:02.220 --> 00:33:07.380
people in the planet who seems to recognize&nbsp;
it as hypomania and mania, but so we've got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.380 --> 00:33:13.260
lot of work to do in terms of understanding it.
I'll give you an evolutionary speculation about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.260 --> 00:33:20.400
what I think is happening, widely speculative.&nbsp;
We don't have the research for this, but I've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:20.400 --> 00:33:26.220
seen this a lot. Not just with ketogenic diet,&nbsp;
but with anything that induces a kind of ketosis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:34.260
and a fasting state or a fasting mimicking state.&nbsp;
So I've seen this in patients who fast for five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:42.960
seven days or whatever, and there are actually&nbsp;
some studies in Muslim patients during Ramadan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:49.680
especially patients who have bipolar disorder&nbsp;
during Ramadan, they start fasting, they're much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.680 --> 00:33:55.380
much more likely to have sleep problems and they&nbsp;
are much, much more likely to have subsequent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:55.380 --> 00:34:01.020
mood episodes, but primarily hypomanic and&nbsp;
manic, but also depressive for some people.&nbsp;

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:07.560
So I think that we've got enough evidence, and I&nbsp;
certainly have enough anecdotal evidence to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:14.940
decreased sleep is a thing, it's a real thing,&nbsp;
it's observed, we've got some studies to document&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:21.720
it. Why would the body need less sleep or&nbsp;
why would the body get less sleep while in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:27.360
a ketogenic state? My assumption is just going&nbsp;
with a common sense evolutionary perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:35.040
which is the body thinks it's actually starving,&nbsp;
it's drought, there's a drought or famine or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:40.800
something. Something is going on. Food supply&nbsp;
is scarce. That's why you have high ketones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:48.060
And so the high ketones are actually activating&nbsp;
brain regions to get you number one, more energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:56.160
more confidence, and more time, you don't need to&nbsp;
be sleeping right now, you've got to find food and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:56.160 --> 00:35:03.900
find it fast. And so it gives people more energy&nbsp;
to actually go out, seek, explore, hunt, whatever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:12.660
looking for food sources. Again, wild speculation,&nbsp;
but at least in my mind it kind of makes sense.

00:35:12.660 --> 00:35:20.520
Tim Ferriss: From an evolutionary perspective, definitely it makes sense and does&nbsp;
the mood improvement reflect, in your opinion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.360 --> 00:35:30.780
increased release of norepinephrine or something&nbsp;
along those lines? Or is it remedying via ketones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:35.220
this alternate fuel source which the brain and&nbsp;
the heart and some other tissues really love?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.600 --> 00:35:44.520
Is the presence of ketones, remedy and underlying&nbsp;
glucose metabolism disorder that is in part causal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:51.840
with the conditions that end up getting&nbsp;
pulled from the DSM-5? How would you explain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:59.400
even if it's just a tentative hypothesis, the mood&nbsp;
elevation that people experience? Because it is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:05.615
noticeable, it is not subtle for me in the least.
Dr. Christopher Palmer: Yeah, no, and it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.615 --> 00:36:10.860
been — I will share my personal story too,&nbsp;
because I've been in and out of ketosis for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:10.860 --> 00:36:17.940
many years and I find the exact same thing. And&nbsp;
quite honestly, sometimes the sleep, the inability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:24.120
to sleep or sleep inadequate amount of time is&nbsp;
the rate limiting factor for me. And I'm like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:30.720
I can't go with this little sleep for this long.&nbsp;
This is not going to turn out well for me if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:36.660
stay on this trajectory, I know enough. So there&nbsp;
are lots of people with lots of different opinions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:42.120
and we know at least 40 different mechanisms&nbsp;
of action of the ketogenic diet, like I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:47.820
neurotransmitters, all sorts of things, so how&nbsp;
exactly is it resulting in this improved mood?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:57.180
There's going to be debate. I actually firmly&nbsp;
