00:56:43.440 --> 00:56:46.860
Dr. Christopher Palmer: Well, and I think you&nbsp;
raise a really good point that not everybody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:46.860 --> 00:56:53.700
should be or needs to be on a ketogenic diet or&nbsp;
in ketosis long term, but for the patients that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:58.260
I'm working with, they usually have serious&nbsp;
brain disorders that almost all of them are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:58.260 --> 00:57:06.180
disabled by their symptoms. And so like epilepsy,&nbsp;
where somebody's having multiple seizures every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:06.180 --> 00:57:12.960
single day, they have a very serious brain&nbsp;
disorder that needs immediate and consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:12.960 --> 00:57:19.380
medical intervention. And so if you're using&nbsp;
a ketogenic diet in those situations, most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:19.380 --> 00:57:25.140
those patients do need it consistently. And it&nbsp;
in a way, I know people want a one-size-fits-all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:25.140 --> 00:57:30.060
answer. And I have to tell you, I have just&nbsp;
have to level with you, there isn't one.&nbsp;

00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:41.220
With somebody who's really obese, say, weighs&nbsp;
340 pounds, I might use carb restriction alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:41.220 --> 00:57:49.260
So tell them to just eat less than 20 grams of&nbsp;
carbs a day. I don't really even have to get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:49.260 --> 00:57:54.420
any limits on protein and I usually don't have to&nbsp;
recommend that they eat more fat or fat sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:54.420 --> 00:58:01.320
because they have so much body fat to tap into.&nbsp;
And usually in those cases, we want to tap into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.320 --> 00:58:06.720
that body fat because they're usually interested&nbsp;
in losing some of it. And so in those people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:06.720 --> 00:58:12.900
I'm going to recommend carb restriction and&nbsp;
that's about it. And usually, it's pretty easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:12.900 --> 00:58:18.960
for them to maintain very high levels of ketones&nbsp;
and get the therapeutic brain effects as a result.&nbsp;

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:26.700
Once that same person loses enough of that&nbsp;
body fat, or if I have a different patient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.700 --> 00:58:34.320
coming to me who's already thin or athletic,&nbsp;
that person is going to need to probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.000 --> 00:58:42.240
moderate the amount of protein they're&nbsp;
eating and consume more fat than they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:42.240 --> 00:58:47.280
really think they should be eating.
Tim Ferriss: We're both laughing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:47.280 --> 00:58:51.180
because I know what that means. Yeah.
Dr. Christopher Palmer: Yeah. No. When I introduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:51.180 --> 00:58:54.900
people to a ketogenic diet and I tell them how&nbsp;
