01:05:34.560 --> 01:05:41.040
associated disorders in the short run. Could you&nbsp;
speak to that, maybe give examples because this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:42.300 --> 01:05:46.500
certainly deeply interesting to me.
Dr. Christopher Palmer: Yeah. So let me at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:52.020
preface this discussion with kind of a caution up&nbsp;
front. So I'm going to say some things that may&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:52.020 --> 01:06:00.120
get people really upset, and especially if you're&nbsp;
on these psychiatric medications. And I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:05.460
just say for the record, it is really difficult&nbsp;
and dangerous to get off psychiatric medications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:05.460 --> 01:06:11.820
especially if you've been on them for years or&nbsp;
decades. So please do not stop your medication&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:11.820 --> 01:06:17.640
on your own. Please don't taper it on your own.&nbsp;
Work with your healthcare professional and do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:17.640 --> 01:06:23.580
in a safe way. You're going to hear that I am&nbsp;
an advocate of sometimes trying to help people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:28.680
get off medication. So I'm not trying to say you&nbsp;
must take your meds for the rest of your life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:28.680 --> 01:06:32.940
but I'm just saying please don't do it in&nbsp;
a dangerous way because lots of people end&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:32.940 --> 01:06:38.220
up in the hospital or suicidal or dead.
Tim Ferriss: No DIY folks, please. I know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:38.220 --> 01:06:43.620
people who've committed suicide after stopping&nbsp;
things cold turkey or attempting to taper but&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.620 --> 01:06:50.280
not informing their doctors. So please be very&nbsp;
careful and only do any type of removal of meds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:50.280 --> 01:06:53.880
with professional supervision. Thank you.
Dr. Christopher Palmer: So with that said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:53.880 --> 01:07:01.740
this is probably going to be the single&nbsp;
most controversial part of the theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:03.120 --> 01:07:08.280
because ultimately what I'm saying is that mental&nbsp;
disorders are metabolic disorders of the brain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:09.180 --> 01:07:16.980
And so that means metabolic impairment in&nbsp;
the body and/or brain can result in mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:16.980 --> 01:07:24.360
symptoms. And yet we know many of our treatments&nbsp;
directly impair metabolism. So we know a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:24.360 --> 01:07:29.460
of antipsychotics, mood stabilizers,&nbsp;
even some antidepressants and others&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:30.360 --> 01:07:37.200
cause people to gain weight, sometimes massive&nbsp;
amounts of weight. They can cause type 2 diabetes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:37.200 --> 01:07:43.560
they can cause cardiovascular disease and all of&nbsp;
the risk factors for cardiovascular disease. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:43.560 --> 01:07:50.040
they can cause hypertension, they can cause&nbsp;
abnormal lipids which we think contribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.040 --> 01:07:55.020
to cardiovascular disease. And those are all&nbsp;
metabolic problems. That's not controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.020 --> 01:08:00.540
at all, their metabolic problems. These pills&nbsp;
are causing these metabolic problems. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.540 --> 01:08:09.000
right on the FDA package inserts of these meds. So&nbsp;
everything I just said is fact, non-controversial.&nbsp;

01:08:09.780 --> 01:08:14.940
So the logical question from many people is,&nbsp;
"Well then, Chris, your theory must be wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:24.000
because these pills work. And&nbsp;
if they're impairing metabolism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:24.000 --> 01:08:30.720
then that pretty much disproves your theory.&nbsp;
How would this make sense otherwise?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:32.820 --> 01:08:36.360
And quite honestly, this was actually one&nbsp;
of the biggest things I struggled with in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:36.360 --> 01:08:43.260
developing this theory. I kind of was on this&nbsp;
path, metabolism mitochondria, wow, that seems to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:43.260 --> 01:08:48.960
be the connecting that's connecting a lot of dots.&nbsp;
And that was actually one of the obstacles for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.960 --> 01:08:55.200
was that exact question. How can I understand&nbsp;
that? And it's not that I want to deny that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:55.200 --> 01:08:59.940
the medications do reduce symptoms because they&nbsp;
do reduce symptoms. I've seen it work firsthand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.940 --> 01:09:06.120
as a psychiatrist. I've seen these medications&nbsp;
reduce symptoms and I'm not here to say all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:12.300
the clinical trials are a big lie and scam from&nbsp;
pharmaceutical companies. I don't think they are.&nbsp;

01:09:14.580 --> 01:09:20.640
So I had to try to understand, well, then&nbsp;
what's going on? It kind of does disprove&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.640 --> 01:09:24.900
my theory. So I've got to come up with an&nbsp;
explanation. And at the end of the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:25.980 --> 01:09:34.680
the explanation is this dichotomous reaction&nbsp;
of a cell to metabolic impairment. So when a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.680 --> 01:09:40.860
cell is metabolically compromised, there are some&nbsp;
other things that it can do. But the two for our&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:40.860 --> 01:09:48.000
purposes right now are underactive or overactive&nbsp;
or hyperexcitable. And what we're really focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:55.620
on are hyperexcitable cells. So hyperexcitable&nbsp;
cells are producing experiences from your brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:55.620 --> 01:10:02.580
networks, but they're producing these experiences&nbsp;
at the wrong time or the wrong intensity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:03.660 --> 01:10:09.360
or they're not stopping when they should. That's&nbsp;
what hyperexcitability does. So a hyperexcitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:09.360 --> 01:10:15.960
pain cell causes pain when you shouldn't have&nbsp;
pain. A hyperexcitable brain cell is producing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:15.960 --> 01:10:22.080
an experience sensation, cognition, something&nbsp;
that should not be happening when it happens.&nbsp;

01:10:22.080 --> 01:10:28.800
And that could be anxiety,&nbsp;
depression, hallucinations, whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.480 --> 01:10:35.640
In order to stop a hyperexcitable cell,&nbsp;
there are kind of two strategies you can use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:36.660 --> 01:10:43.800
So one would be to slowly but surely restore the&nbsp;
metabolic health of that cell through strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:43.800 --> 01:10:54.720
that Tim, you've used yourself, exercise, ketones,&nbsp;
fasting, meditation, other things. Those are going&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:54.720 --> 01:10:59.580
to restore metabolic health to those cells and&nbsp;
allow those cells to function normally again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:00.600 --> 01:11:09.300
But the other way to stop a hyperexcitable cell is&nbsp;
to basically put a straitjacket on it, and to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:09.300 --> 01:11:18.420
it from functioning almost altogether. And that's&nbsp;
what a lot of the pills that we prescribe are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:18.420 --> 01:11:28.920
doing. They are impairing mitochondrial function&nbsp;
so that that cell has trouble firing at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:36.360
And that means that symptoms of hyperexcitability&nbsp;
can stop. And so for some people, if you're having&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:36.360 --> 01:11:41.580
a panic attack and somebody gives you a pill and&nbsp;
it makes that stop, that can feel really good.&nbsp;

01:11:42.660 --> 01:11:49.440
If you're having a seizure, it can be life&nbsp;
saving. If you're psychotic and violent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:49.440 --> 01:11:55.740
that can be lifesaving. So I'm not trying to&nbsp;
say these pills should never be used or they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:55.740 --> 01:12:01.380
don't have a time and a place because I think&nbsp;
they do. But if you take that pill long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.340 --> 01:12:08.520
that pill is like putting a straitjacket on&nbsp;
that cell, it's suppressing the function of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:08.520 --> 01:12:15.600
that cell over the long run. And what that means&nbsp;
is that that cell becomes weaker over the long run&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.680 --> 01:12:23.700
and that may very well end up making&nbsp;
the illness even worse in the long run.&nbsp;

01:12:24.720 --> 01:12:30.840
That is the theory. There are lots of people who&nbsp;
have been preaching that message for decades who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:30.840 --> 01:12:34.800
are going to say, "Dr. Chris Palmer, where the&nbsp;
hell have you been?" And then there are other&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:34.800 --> 01:12:39.840
people who are going to say, "Chris Palmer,&nbsp;
how dare you say that? That is blasphemy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:39.840 --> 01:12:44.760
you are now a dangerous quack." So I know I'm&nbsp;
going to get responses from all over the place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:45.420 --> 01:12:50.520
but if you understand the science, if you&nbsp;
understand the basic science and all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:50.520 --> 01:12:59.160
the evidence, that is a real possibility.
Tim Ferriss: Are there any, let's just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:59.160 --> 01:13:02.340
I don't want to say worst offenders, because&nbsp;
that has too much of a negative connotation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:02.340 --> 01:13:08.040
but are there any particular drugs that stand&nbsp;
out to you as having a time and a place to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:08.040 --> 01:13:17.940
be used but then are particularly powerful at&nbsp;
disrupting metabolic integrity if they're used&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:17.940 --> 01:13:24.960
chronically without any type of cycling off?
Dr. Christopher Palmer: The easy answer is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.960 --> 01:13:30.660
and I could get into all of the science&nbsp;
documenting at the cellular level how and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:30.660 --> 01:13:35.640
why this has been supported, but we really&nbsp;
don't need that. The easy answer is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:35.640 --> 01:13:39.960
look at the human being in front of you. If&nbsp;
they are gaining massive amounts of weight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:40.920 --> 01:13:48.120
if they feel extraordinarily slowed down and now&nbsp;
they're sleeping 14 hours a day, 16 hours a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:48.900 --> 01:13:56.400
and they can barely function and their cognition&nbsp;
is trashed and they can't think anymore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:56.400 --> 01:14:01.560
they can't read a book anymore, they&nbsp;
can't hold a job, they can't do school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:02.640 --> 01:14:06.660
Those are all signs of metabolic&nbsp;
impairment of brain cells.&nbsp;

01:14:06.660 --> 01:14:13.080
So those are the medications that I would be most&nbsp;
worried about, if somebody's taking — and it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.080 --> 01:14:19.680
differ among people. If one person is taking a&nbsp;
medication and having all of those side effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.680 --> 01:14:24.960
that I just listed, and yet another person is&nbsp;
taking maybe a lower dose of that same medication&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:26.040 --> 01:14:32.460
and is finding it beneficial and still&nbsp;
able to function in life and still able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:32.460 --> 01:14:36.960
to do everything they want to do and not gaining&nbsp;
lots of weight and not becoming type 2 diabetic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:37.560 --> 01:14:42.240
then all the power to that person and all the&nbsp;
